{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: corner the parity harness doesn't pin — awk `&` in the replacement text**

The JS replacement uses a function, so the tail lands verbatim:

```js
next = next.replace(STARTED_TAIL_RE, function () {
    return st + ' · done ' + endTs + ' ' + mark;
});
```

POSIX awk `sub()` treats `&` in the replacement string as «the matched text», so if `mark` or `endTs` ever contained `&`, the sh output would expand it while the JS lands a literal `&` — a genuine (if exotic) spec divergence the 2000/2000 harness run can't see, because `MARKS`/`hhmm()` never generate `&`.

Reachability is near-zero (`mark` is ✅/❌/🚫 or `⚠️ <JOB_RESULT>` with `JOB_RESULT` workflow-controlled to success/failure/cancelled), so no code change is required. Cheapest closure: add an `&`-bearing entry to `MARKS` (e.g. `'⚠️ a&b'`) and either pin the divergence deliberately (if kept, with a comment) or escape it. Fine to defer to the parity-harness polish bucket with comment3.
